#> tool: sedrepl
#> wait: # command:  sed -n -f
#> pause: 45
#> must: In Xanadu did Kubla Khan
#> must: A stately pleasure dome decree:
#> must: Through caverns measureless to man
#> must: Down to a sunless sea.
#> not: Where Alph
#> source: \(an\).*\1
# 6. The pieces of a pattern. McMahon 2.2, items 1 to 11
# [AT] matches one character from the set, so /^[AT]/ selects lines that start
# with A or T. \(an\) remembers what it matched, and \1 demands the same text
# again later in the line: /\(an\).*\1/ selects line 1 alone. An empty pattern,
# //, reuses the last one compiled; /\.$/ wants a period at the very end.
# Try: change [AT] to [^AT]; then change \1 to \(an\) and see nothing change.
#ARGS -n
/^[AT]/p
/\(an\).*\1/p
/\.$/p

#DATA
In Xanadu did Kubla Khan
A stately pleasure dome decree:
Where Alph, the sacred river, ran
Through caverns measureless to man
Down to a sunless sea.
